#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093400 :tutorial/content "Consider the following geometric sequence: \n\n$$\n3,\\quad 6,\\quad 12,\\quad 24, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose we want to find the first term that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$ We can do this by creating and solving an inequality.\n\nFirst, let's construct a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence. The first term of the sequence is $a_1=3,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 6 3 = 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $3\\,000\\,000.$ So, we can set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 3\\cdot {2}^{n-1} & >3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{n-1} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\,000)} {\\log (2)} + 1 \\approx 20.932.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $21$st term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$\n\nTo check our answer, we can easily verify that $a_{20} < 3\\, 000\\, 000$ and $a_{21} > 3\\, 000\\, 000\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{20} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{20-1}=3\\cdot {2}^{19}=1\\,572\\,864<3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {a}_{21} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{21-1}=3\\cdot {2}^{20}=3\\,145\\,728>3\\,000\\,000\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $a_{21}$ is indeed the first term that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9a55e4c5-cd46-5a39-97aa-f29fe4e37185" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2886 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
